A client's transport errors as quic-go closes on them.

quic-go puts three things in the CONNECTION_CLOSE a transport error ends the connection with: the
code, the type of the frame the error happened on, and its own message as the reason
(vendor/quic-go/packet_packer.go:202-208). ngtcp2 returned a liberr, from which the client inferred
a code; the frame type went out as 0 and the reason empty. And several checks were not quic-go's
at all.

Every check a server's packet can fail now records, where it fails, what quic-go says at the same
point (conn->qgo_close, ngtcp2_conn_qgo_close), and the client closes with it
(ngtcp2_conn_get_qgo_ccerr); the Frame Type field is written from the ngtcp2_ccerr. Where quic-go
decides differently, the client decides as quic-go:

- frames are parsed as FrameParser does: PADDING skipped, the type a varint of any length, an
  unknown type and one not allowed at the level refused as FRAME_ENCODING_ERROR before the body is
  read (upstream: PROTOCOL_VIOLATION after decoding it); ACK, STREAM, MAX_STREAMS, STREAMS_BLOCKED,
  NEW_CONNECTION_ID and NEW_TOKEN bodies checked in quic-go's order, with its words;
- an error in an Initial packet closes the connection, as in any other packet (upstream dropped
  the packet); a packet's reserved bits and an empty payload are refused before a duplicate is
  dropped;
- an ACK is refused for the four skipped packet numbers quic-go remembers, the lowest first;
- CRYPTO: 16 KiB counted on empty frames too, new data after a level's stream is finished refused,
  and data left over when the next keys arrive;
- streams found as streamsMap does (the credit granted, not the credit sent; RESET_STREAM on our
  own unidirectional stream is STREAM_STATE_ERROR), the final size checked before the windows, and
  STREAM_DATA_BLOCKED, DATA_BLOCKED and STREAMS_BLOCKED taken without checks;
- NEW_CONNECTION_ID and RETIRE_CONNECTION_ID as connIDManager.Add and connIDGenerator.Retire;
- PATH_RESPONSE refused (hysteria turns quic-go's path manager off); DATAGRAM refused over 16383;
- KEY_UPDATE_ERROR for keys updated too quickly and for an ACK the peer's keys never followed;
- no glitch limit; 20000 gaps in reassembly, none in acknowledged ranges;
- transport parameters parsed as Unmarshal (refusals are INTERNAL_ERROR with
  "TRANSPORT_PARAMETER_ERROR (local): ..." as the reason, what quic-go's TLS layer makes of them),
  version_information and grease_quic_bit not read, and checkTransportParameters in place of
  upstream's client checks;
- a stateless reset answered with the CONNECTION_CLOSE quic-go sends for it;
- a NEW_CONNECTION_ID reordered or already retired answered with a RETIRE_CONNECTION_ID every time,
  as connIDManager.add queues one (upstream: once per sequence number, tracked and bounded);
- more control frames queued than maxControlFrames (vendor/quic-go/framer.go:18, :119-122), 16384,
  close with INTERNAL_ERROR, no frame type and no reason, asked at the top of each step as the run
  loop asks QueuedTooManyControlFrames (vendor/quic-go/connection.go:616-619) — here
  ngtcp2_conn_qgo_check_control_frames;
- a received ACK read for all its ranges, as parseAckFrame reads them (upstream: 63, which is what
  this end sends since 0029, and the rest skipped).

The server keeps upstream's code. Checked against quic-go's own code, run by tools/qgovec:
test/ngpatch.

